{% block usage %}
## Usage

### Features

- Curve-driven fan control over hwmon (motherboard/CPU headers) and four Corsair
  device families: Commander Core, Commander Pro, Corsair PSU, and Hydro Platinum
- NVIDIA GPU fan control via NVML
- A REST and WebSocket API served alongside an embedded web UI, no separate
  frontend to install
- Curve types: point, trigger, target, flat, mix, and sync
- Virtual sensors: max, min, mean (optionally smoothed over a window), offset and
  delta nodes that combine or transform sensors before a curve reads them, plus
  webhook nodes that take their value from an HTTP POST
- Graph editor: one canvas per profile wiring device sensors, virtual sensors, curves
  and device controls together, with live values on every port and edge
- Journaled claims so a killed daemon restores prior fan state on the next start

### Quick start

`galed` reads its config from the path in `GALE_CONFIG` (default
`/etc/gale/config.toml`). A minimal config assigning a point curve to a
motherboard PWM header:

```toml
tick_interval_ms = 200
active_profile = "default"

[api]
bind = "127.0.0.1:5250"

[profiles.default.curves.cpu]
type = "point"
sensor = "hwmon/nct6798/temp1"
points = [[30.0, 20.0], [70.0, 100.0]]

[profiles.default.assignments]
"hwmon/nct6798/pwm1" = "cpu"
```

Start the daemon (or `systemctl start galed` once installed) and open
`http://127.0.0.1:5250` for the dashboard and graph editor, or drive it with
the `gale` CLI.

### Graph editor

The Graph page is the primary way to build a profile. Every hardware sensor and
control shows up automatically as a node with one handle per channel; dragging an
edge from a sensor into a curve or virtual sensor wires it, and dragging an edge
into a control handle is the assignment, replacing the old assignment table.
Curves, virtual sensors and mix or sync nodes sit between the two, so a max of two
temperature probes feeding a point curve that drives three fans is a handful of
drags rather than three separate forms. Node positions are saved with the profile
under `[ui.graph.<profile>]` so a layout survives a reinstall. Saving applies the
whole profile through the same validation the API always enforced, and any warning
comes back attached to the node it concerns.

### Virtual sensors

A profile can define derived sensors under `[profiles.<name>.sensors]`. They are
addressed as `virtual/<id>` anywhere a sensor id is accepted, including other
virtual sensors, and show up in the dashboard and `GET /api/status` like any
hardware sensor.

```toml
[profiles.default.sensors.cpu_hot]
type = "max"
inputs = ["superio/nct6798d/temp2", "nvidia/0/temp"]

[profiles.default.sensors.coolant_smooth]
type = "mean"
inputs = ["corsair/commander-pro-0805009c9327/temp1"]
window_s = 10

[profiles.default.sensors.coolant_rise]
type = "delta"
input = "virtual/coolant_smooth"
window_s = 30

[profiles.default.sensors.gpu_adjusted]
type = "offset"
input = "nvidia/0/temp"
add = -5.0
scale = 1.0

[profiles.default.curves.case]
type = "point"
sensor = "virtual/cpu_hot"
points = [[35.0, 25.0], [75.0, 100.0]]
```

| type | inputs | output |
| ------ | ------ | ------ |
| `max`, `min` | one or more sensor ids | the extreme of the inputs that have a value; unavailable only when every input is |
| `mean` | one or more ids, optional `window_s` | the mean of the available inputs, smoothed over `window_s` seconds when set |
| `offset` | one id, `add`, `scale` | `input * scale + add` |
| `delta` | one id, `window_s` | change in degrees per minute over the window, positive when rising |
| `webhook` | none; `token`, optional `timeout_s` | the last value received by `POST /api/webhook/<token>`; unavailable until the first POST and, when `timeout_s` is set, after that many seconds without one |

A virtual sensor with no value behaves like an unplugged probe: the curve reading it
has no input and its fan falls back to 100 percent. Cycles between virtual sensors
and references to undefined ones are rejected when the config is loaded. Missing
hardware behind a virtual sensor is reported in `GET /api/warnings` by its hardware
id.

### Webhook sensors

A `webhook` sensor is a virtual sensor with no upstream inputs: a script, Home
Assistant, or any other service pushes a reading into it over HTTP and it behaves
like a hardware probe from then on.

```toml
[profiles.default.sensors.room]
type = "webhook"
token = "replace-me-with-the-output-of-openssl-rand-hex-32"
timeout_s = 120.0
```

```sh
curl -X POST http://127.0.0.1:5250/api/webhook/replace-me-with-the-output-of-openssl-rand-hex-32 \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '{"value": 23.5}'
```

The body is `{"value": <number>}` or `{"value": <bool>}`; `true` maps to `1.0` and
`false` to `0.0`, so a boolean feeding a 0 to 100 duty range goes through an
`offset` node with `scale = 100.0`. The token in the URL is the only credential, the
same model Discord and GitHub use for incoming webhooks: the endpoint ignores
`[api] api_key`, an unknown token answers 404, and the daemon binds to
`127.0.0.1` by default so the URL is only reachable from the machine unless you
change `[api] bind`. Creating the node in the graph editor generates a 64
character token on the first save and shows the full URL with a copy button in the
node's panel (`GET /api/webhook-url/<name>` behind the API key). When writing the
config by hand, put any long random string in `token`, for example the output of
`openssl rand -hex 32`. Without `timeout_s` the last value never expires; with it,
the sensor reads as unavailable once that many seconds pass without a POST, and
any curve reading it falls back to 100 percent through the usual path. Values are
not kept across a daemon restart.

### API

The daemon exposes REST and WebSocket endpoints under `/api`, all guarded by
an optional API key set in `[api] api_key`, except the webhook endpoint, which
is guarded by its own token:

| Method | Path                      | Description                          |
| ------ | ------------------------- | ------------------------------------ |
| GET    | `/api/status`             | Daemon status                        |
| GET    | `/api/inventory`          | Detected sensors and controls, plus the active profile's virtual sensors |
| GET    | `/api/config`             | Current config                       |
| PUT    | `/api/config`             | Replace and reload the config        |
| GET    | `/api/warnings`           | Non-fatal startup and runtime issues |
| POST   | `/api/profiles/:name/activate` | Switch the active profile       |
| PUT    | `/api/controls/*id`       | Set a manual override duty           |
| DELETE | `/api/controls/*id`       | Clear a manual override              |
| POST   | `/api/webhook/:token`     | Push `{"value": <number or bool>}` into a webhook sensor; the token is the credential, no API key |
| GET    | `/api/webhook-url/:name`  | Full POST URL for a webhook sensor, built from the request's Host header |
| GET    | `/api/ws`                 | WebSocket stream of live sensor/control state |
